 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 0,
   "end": 2.48,
   "text": "Never eat from your fridge after a blackout..."
  },
  {
   "start": 2.93,
   "end": 4.05,
   "text": "until you know this."
  },
  {
   "start": 4.44,
   "end": 7.55,
   "text": "The moment the power comes back is when people get sick."
  },
  {
   "start": 7.95,
   "end": 9.33,
   "text": "Your fridge is humming again."
  },
  {
   "start": 9.65,
   "end": 10.93,
   "text": "The food feels cold."
  },
  {
   "start": 11.27,
   "end": 12.36,
   "text": "So people eat it."
  },
  {
   "start": 12.74,
   "end": 13.79,
   "text": "That's the mistake."
  },
  {
   "start": 14.15,
   "end": 18.94,
   "text": "The USDA says food in a fridge stays safe for only about four hours without power."
  },
  {
   "start": 19.24,
   "end": 21.04,
   "text": "After that, meat, milk,"
  },
  {
   "start": 21.2,
   "end": 23.03,
   "text": "eggs and leftovers have to go."
  },
  {
   "start": 23.41,
   "end": 24.57,
   "text": "Your freezer is different."
  },
  {
   "start": 24.85,
   "end": 27.59,
   "text": "If the food still has ice crystals, you can refreeze it."
  },
  {
   "start": 27.9,
   "end": 30.14,
   "text": "And never taste food to check if it's safe."
  },
  {
   "start": 30.47,
   "end": 32.15,
   "text": "The USDA's rule is simple:"
  },
  {
   "start": 32.53,
   "end": 33.12,
   "text": "when in doubt..."
  },
  {
   "start": 33.42,
   "end": 34.12,
   "text": "throw it out."
  },
  {
   "start": 34.64,
   "end": 37.22,
   "text": "So how does a dead power grid come back to life?"
  },
  {
   "start": 37.65,
   "end": 38.24,
   "text": "That's next."
  },
  {
   "start": 38.57,
   "end": 39.59,
   "text": "Follow for part eight."
  }
 ]
}